## Side effects and initialization order (important)

The original runs top to bottom, so the creation order was implicit. With modules, **any module-level code runs at import time**, which would create the WebGL renderer before the fallback checks. To avoid that:

- Pure modules (config, math, noise, color, fields, geometry, assets) may compute things at import time, just as the original did (e.g. `PAL`, `LEAF` and `N2` are built when the module loads). `rgb()` uses `T.Color`, so these modules depend on `js/three.js`, and `main.js` must confirm `window.THREE` exists before importing them. Do this by having `main.js` run the THREE check first and then load everything else with a dynamic `import()`, or with an equivalent approach that you explain.
- `render/context.js` must **not** create the renderer at import time. Export an `initRenderer()` function (or similar) that does exactly what section 5 does (try/catch around `new WebGLRenderer`, `outputEncoding`, tone mapping, shadow settings, appending the canvas to `#stage`, the `webglcontextlost` handler, the scene, background, fog, camera, hemi/sun/bounce lights, and the light-space axes) and returns `false` if the renderer throws. Export the created objects through live `export let` bindings or a shared context object.
- `pools.js` must export `buildPools()` and call it only after the scene and materials exist, keeping the same order as the original: renderer and scene, then materials, then pools.
- `main.js` then calls `start(...)` through the hot hook exactly as before.

Watch the **circular references** and keep them call-time only, never import-time:
- `Rig.resize()` reads `Perf.pr`, and `Perf.tick()` calls `Rig.resize()`.
- `Input` writes `Rig.want`, and `Rig` reads `Player`.
- `Player.update` calls `World.collide`. `World` uses `scene`, `pools`, `makeChunk`.
- `Input.init` references `KEYMAP`, which in the original is declared after `Input`. Keep it in the same module or import it.

ES modules handle these cycles as long as nothing touches an imported binding while modules are still evaluating. Check that this holds.

Mutable module state (`shadowExt`, `last`, `time`, `World.*`, `Rig.*`, `Perf.*`) must keep exactly one owner. Don't copy a primitive into another module and expect it to stay in sync.

